---
import Prose from './Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro';
import { NODE_TOOLS } from '../data/guide';
---

<Prose class="max-w-none">
  <p>
    TechCart pins its Node version in two one-line files at the repository root, both containing the
    single line <code>24</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock title="root" code={'$ cat .nvmrc .node-version\n24\n24'} />

  <h3>Which tool reads which</h3>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-surface text-xs tracking-wide text-muted uppercase">
        <tr>
          <th class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">File</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Read by</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Switch command</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {NODE_TOOLS.map((tool) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border align-top">
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono whitespace-nowrap text-fg">{tool.file}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 text-muted">{tool.readBy}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-muted">{tool.command}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Why pin</h3>
  <p>
    So development, CI and production all run the same Node major, and the class of &ldquo;works on
    my machine&rdquo; bug that comes from version drift simply disappears. TechCart's CI reads the
    pin with the setup-node option <code>node-version-file: .nvmrc</code>, and Vercel and Render
    honour these files too, so the whole pipeline agrees on one runtime.
  </p>

  <h3>Best practice</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>commit both files;</li>
    <li>keep them in sync &mdash; a mismatch is worse than no pin;</li>
    <li>pin at least the major version;</li>
    <li>bump the version through a normal PR so CI validates the change before it lands.</li>
  </ul>
</Prose>
